The maximum number of edges in a connected graph with \(2n\) vertices (\(n>1\)) for which Alice can evade Bob indefinitely is:

\[
\boxed{
\begin{cases}
n^2 & \text{if } n = 2 \text{ or } n = 3,\\[6pt]
2n(n-1) & \text{if } n \ge 4 \text{ and } n \text{ is even},\\[6pt]
2n^2 - 2n - 1 & \text{if } n \ge 5 \text{ and } n \text{ is odd}.
\end{cases}
}
\]

For \(n=2\) the value \(4\) coincides with the even formula, and for \(n=3\) the value \(9\) is an exception to the odd formula.

The given conditions imply polynomial divisibility, leading to the equations \(Q^2 + aQ + 1 = PR\) and \(P^2 + bP + 1 = QS\), where \(R\) and \(S\) are monic polynomials. Analyzing resultants shows that \(\deg P\) and \(\deg Q\) must be even. Small degrees like 1 and 2 force \(|a|\) or \(|b| = 2\), violating \(|a|, |b| \ge 3\). The smallest even degree allowing \(|a|, |b| \ge 3\) is 4, with higher even degrees also possible. The ten smallest possible degrees are thus 4, 6, 8, …, 22, summing to \(S_2 = 130\).

For \(|a + b|\), assuming symmetry and \(a = b\) gives \(|a + b| = 2|a|\). The smallest \(|a| \ge 3\) is 3, yielding \(|a + b| = 6\), then 8, 10, …, 24. The ten smallest values sum to \(S_1 = 150\). Thus, \(S_1 + S_2 = 280\).

\boxed{280}

