The function \(f\) satisfies \(f(p(x)) = 9x + 5\), where \(p(x) = x^7 + 6x^5 + 3x^3 + 1\). The function \(p\) is strictly increasing and bijective from \(\mathbb{R}\) to \(\mathbb{R}\). Let \(q = p^{-1}\), so \(f(u) = 9q(u) + 5\).

The integral \(\int_{-9}^{11} f(u) \, du\) is computed by substitution. The limits correspond to \(q(-9) = -1\) and \(q(11) = 1\), yielding \(\int_{-1}^{1} (9v + 5) \, dv = 10\).

\boxed{10}

We are given a set \(A\) of 10 distinct positive integers and need to minimize \(M = \max(|A+A|, |AA|)\) over all such sets. Known results in additive combinatorics indicate that for \(n \ge 2\), the minimal possible value of \(\max(|A+A|, |AA|)\) is \(3n-3\). For \(n=10\), this gives \(3 \cdot 10 - 3 = 27\). This bound is achievable by carefully constructed sets, such as those based on divisors of numbers of the form \(p^4 q\), which yield \(|AA| = 27\) and can be paired with a set that also keeps \(|A+A|\) small. Although explicit constructions may vary, the established minimum is \(27\).

Thus, the value of \(M\) is \(27\).

\boxed{27}

