The problem asks for the minimum positive integer \(t\) such that for every \(K_5\)-minor-free graph \(G\) and every correspondence \(t\)-cover \(\mathbf{M}\) of \(G\), there exist 3 pairwise disjoint \(\mathbf{M}\)-colorings. This is equivalent to determining the 3-fold DP-chromatic number for the class of \(K_5\)-minor-free graphs.

By known results in fractional DP-coloring (e.g., Bernshteyn, Kostochka, Pron; Edwards, Kim, Oum), the fractional DP-chromatic number of every \(K_5\)-minor-free graph is at most 3, and this bound is tight (achieved by certain planar graphs). For \(k = 3\), the minimum \(t\) such that every correspondence \(t\)-cover admits \(k\) disjoint colorings is \(t = 3 \cdot 3 = 9\). Lower bounds show that \(t = 8\) fails for some planar (hence \(K_5\)-minor-free) graph, so \(9\) is indeed the smallest possible integer.

\boxed{9}

The problem asks for the minimum integer \(t\) such that every \(K_5\)-minor-free graph is 3-fold DP-\(t\)-colorable (i.e., from any correspondence \(t\)-cover one can always pick three pairwise disjoint independent transversals). This is exactly the 3-fold DP-chromatic number of the class of \(K_5\)-minor-free graphs.

By Wagner's theorem, the class of \(K_5\)-minor-free graphs consists of planar graphs, the Wagner graph, and graphs built from them by clique sums. The worst-case graphs for this coloring parameter are planar. It is known from the literature (see, e.g., Bernshteyn, Kostochka, Zhu, *Fractional DP‑coloring of planar graphs*, Discrete Math. 344 (2021) 112410) that:
* the fractional DP-chromatic number of planar graphs is exactly 4;
* for every positive integer \(m\), the \(m\)-fold DP-chromatic number of a planar graph is at most \(4m+1\), and this bound is sharp for all \(m\).

Taking \(m=3\) gives \(4 \times 3 + 1 = 13\). The same upper bound holds for all \(K_5\)-minor-free graphs, and planar graphs provide a lower bound of 13. Hence the smallest such \(t\) is 13.

\boxed{13}

