This question refers to results on defective chromatic polynomials of trees. The known result: the family of defective chromatic polynomials of a tree determines the number of paths P_1 through P_6 (i.e., up to 6 vertices), but there exist non-isomorphic trees with the same defective chromatic polynomials for all d that differ in the number of P_7 subgraphs.

Actually, let me recall more carefully. The paper "Defective chromatic polynomials" (or similar) shows that for trees, the collection {χ_d(T;k)} determines the number of vertices, edges, paths P_3, P_4, P_5, and P_6, but there exist trees with identical defective chromatic polynomials for all d differing in N(P_7).

\boxed{6}

